            <ArticleTitle>5-HT1Dbeta receptor mediates the supersensitivity of isolated coronary artery to serotonin in variant angina.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>243-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine; 5-HT) is used for provocation of coronary spasm, 5-HT receptor subtypes in spastic coronary arteries remain undetermined. We demonstrated the supersensitivity of isolated coronary artery to ergonovine, 5-HT, and sumatriptan, a 5-HT1D receptor agonist, in a patient with variant angina. Furthermore, we detected gene expression of 5-HT1Dbeta and 5-HT2A receptors in spastic coronary artery using RNase protection assay. These findings suggest that the leftward shift of the dose-response curve for 5-HT, which plays an important role in the pathogenesis of coronary spasm, is mediated by activation of 5-HT1Dbeta receptor.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
